\DOC genvars

\TYPE {genvars : term list -> hol_type list -> term list}

\SYNOPSIS
Returns variables of given types that are fresh for given terms.

\DESCRIBE
The call {genvars tms tys} returns a list of variables, one for each type in
the list {tys}, named {_v} followed by a number. The numbers are {n},
{n + 1}, ... in order, where {n = genvar_index tms}, so the variables are
distinct from each other and from all the variables, free or bound, in the
terms {tms}, even ones of other types. They are also distinct from all the
variables made by {genvar}, whose names are an underscore followed by a
number. Unlike {genvar}, the result depends only on the arguments, not on any
earlier calls.

\FAILURE
Never fails, except in the absurd case noted under {genvar_index}.

\EXAMPLE
{
  # genvars [`\x _v3:num. _v1 + x`; `_v10 /\ _v007`] [`:num`; `:bool`];;
  val it : term list = [`_v11`; `_v12`]
}

\USES
Writing derived rules that need fresh variables, without the global state of
{genvar}, so that the results of a proof are the same whatever was done
before. If the names are to be visible to a typical user, the function
{variant} can provide rather more meaningful names.

\SEEALSO
genvar, genvar_index, genvar_index_goal, mk_genvar, variant, variants.

\ENDDOC
